The shared chlor-alkali Record supplies the chemistry and process account. Now follow the co-produced outputs to their markets and actual supply arrangements. A user's requirement for one output does not independently set plant production; other outputs, energy, equipment, storage and distribution have their own constraints.

Reconstruct the EPA sodium-hydroxide profile in full, keeping its data years separate from today's conditions. Draw one causal hypothesis for a local shortage and identify the observations that would test it. More distributors may not diversify production if they draw from the same plant. Conversely, a producer can have capacity while a terminal, receiving tank or compatible delivery vehicle limits the user's supply.

Build a simple joint-product account using the actual reaction basis and distinguish theoretical stoichiometry from saleable output and losses. Examine a demand change for one co-product, a planned maintenance period and a transport restriction. State which price, quantity or inventory effects follow from the model and which remain unproven. The purpose is to understand a connected production system, including alternatives and quality, rather than infer a universal market law from one dated disruption case.

